<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01830</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</md:title>
    <md:uuid>dd80717c-4d2d-4b7d-a7d0-8ec2209f8e75</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A cannon is 10 feet off the ground. It launches a cannonball straight up
        with a velocity of 406 feet per second.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Imagine that there is no gravity and that the cannonball continues to
        travel upward with the same velocity.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Answer questions a–f using the table with the heights of the cannonball at different
          times.</item>
      </list>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="2">
        
  <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis> Seconds </emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry><emphasis> Distance above ground (feet) </emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 0 </entry>
              <entry> 10 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
              <entry> a. _____ </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 2 </entry>
              <entry> b. _____ </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 3 </entry>
              <entry> c. _____ </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 4 </entry>
              <entry> d. _____ </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 5 </entry>
              <entry> e. _____ </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
              <entry> f. _____ </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>


        <exercise id="_3a577da6-6105-4e46-85c7-cb4e068e4bfe" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">How far above the ground is the cannonball at 1 second?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Correct! The cannonball is 416 feet above the ground at 1 second.</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is: The cannonball is 416 feet above the ground
              at 1 second.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_6bb155ed-6a1c-4114-9f67-9ad53bae0313" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">How far above the ground is the cannonball at 2 seconds?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Correct! The cannonball is 822 feet above the ground at 2 seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is: The cannonball is 822 feet above the ground
              at 2 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_391c299f-b35d-4c3f-b372-744ba36d15f2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">How far above the ground is the cannonball at 3 seconds?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! The cannonball is 1,228 feet above the ground at 3
              seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is: The cannonball is 1228 feet above the
              ground at 3 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_74878a62-1f11-4e7f-bf75-6d943723008b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00012">How far above the ground is the cannonball at 4 seconds?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00013">Correct! The cannonball is 1634 feet above the ground at 4
              seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is: The cannonball is 1634 feet above the
              ground at 4 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_7676f221-e8dc-4062-b482-97ca000c4044" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00015">How far above the ground is the cannonball at 5 seconds?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00016">Correct! The cannonball is 2040 feet above the ground at 5
              seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is: The cannonball is 2040 feet above the
              ground at 5 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
  
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">How far above the ground is the cannonball at <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> seconds?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">


          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00020">The cannonball is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>406</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> feet above the ground at <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> seconds.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00021">Write an equation to model the distance in feet, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>d</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, of the ball <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> seconds after it was
            fired from the cannon, if there was no gravity.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">


          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00023"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>d</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>406</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>